---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Brand from '../components/Brand.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/Harsh-2002/Orva';

// Every example here is taken from main and is meant to run as written. The
// previous version of this site shipped three handler samples that could not:
// a signature that does not exist, a ctx object that is never passed, and an
// event.body treated as parsed JSON when it has always been a raw string.
const toc = [
  { id: 'start', label: 'Getting started' },
  { id: 'handler', label: 'Handler contract' },
  { id: 'invoke', label: 'Invoking' },
  { id: 'config', label: 'Function settings' },
  { id: 'sdk', label: 'The SDK' },
  { id: 'cli', label: 'CLI' },
  { id: 'server', label: 'Server config' },
  { id: 'security', label: 'Security model' },
  { id: 'mcp', label: 'MCP' },
  { id: 'trouble', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Orva documentation"
  description="How to deploy, invoke, configure and operate Orva: the handler contract, the sandbox SDK, the CLI, server configuration, the security model and the MCP server."
>
  <header class="docs-head">
    <Brand href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL} />
    <h1>Documentation</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Orva runs JavaScript, TypeScript and Python functions inside nsjail sandboxes on hardware you own, and
      serves them over HTTP. This page is enough to deploy something and operate it. The exhaustive reference
      is in the repository.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <div class="toc__in">
        <h2 class="eyebrow">On this page</h2>
        <nav>
          {
            toc.map((t) => (
              <a href={`#${t.id}`} data-toc-link={t.id}>
                {t.label}
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </nav>
        <p class="toc__note faint">
          The canonical reference lives in the repository and ships inside the binary. Run
          <span class="num">orva docs</span>, or read
          <a class="link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/reference.md`}>reference.md</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <article class="body">
      <!-- Getting started -->
      <section id="start">
        <h2>Getting started</h2>
        <p>
          Docker Compose is the shortest path. It publishes the dashboard on host port
          <span class="num">3000</span>, which maps to <span class="num">8443</span> inside the container.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="bash"
          code={`$ curl -fsSL ${'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Harsh-2002/Orva/main/docker-compose.yml'} -o docker-compose.yml
$ docker compose up -d
# http://localhost:3000`}
        />
        <div class="note">
          <p>
            <strong>nsjail is required on Linux.</strong> The server starts without it, but every invocation fails
            until it is installed at
            <span class="num">/usr/local/bin/nsjail</span>. That path is hardcoded: PATH is never consulted
            and there is no environment override. The Docker image ships it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          State is a single directory, <span class="num">/var/lib/orva</span>: one SQLite database in WAL mode
          and a tree of function versions. There is no external database, no control plane and no telemetry.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Handler -->
      <section id="handler">
        <h2>Handler contract</h2>
        <p>
          There are two runtimes, <span class="num">node</span> and
          <span class="num">python</span>, both latest-stable only. TypeScript is a first-class path on <span
            class="num">node</span
          >: it compiles at build time and runs as JavaScript.
        </p>

        <h3>Node.js</h3>
        <CodeBlock
          label="handler.js"
          lang="js"
          code={`exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const body = event.body ? JSON.parse(event.body) : {}

  return {
    statusCode: 200,
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: { hello: body.name || 'world' },
  }
}`}
        />

        <h3>Python</h3>
        <CodeBlock
          label="handler.py"
          lang="python"
          code={`import json


def handler(event):
    raw = event.get("body") or ""
    body = json.loads(raw) if raw else {}

    return {
        "statusCode": 200,
        "headers": {"Content-Type": "application/json"},
        "body": {"hello": body.get("name", "world")},
    }`}
        />

        <div class="note note--warn">
          <p>
            <strong>The body is always a raw string.</strong> The platform never parses it, whatever the <span
              class="num">Content-Type</span
            >. Call
            <span class="num">JSON.parse</span> or <span class="num">json.loads</span> yourself, and guard the empty
            case. Treating it as an already-parsed object is the single most common way a first handler returns
            a 500.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Event and response</h3>
        <p>
          The event carries <span class="num">method</span>, <span class="num">path</span>,
          <span class="num">headers</span> and <span class="num">body</span>. Node additionally gets <span
            class="num">query</span
          >, because its adapter parses it out of the path; Python handlers split <span class="num"
            >event["path"]</span
          > themselves.
        </p>
        <p>
          Return <span class="num">{'{ statusCode, headers, body }'}</span>. A non-string body is JSON-encoded
          by the adapter. Environment variables and secrets arrive in
          <span class="num">process.env</span> and <span class="num">os.environ</span>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Invoke -->
      <section id="invoke">
        <h2>Invoking</h2>
        <p>
          Every function answers at <span class="num">/fn/&lt;function_id&gt;</span>. Friendlier paths are
          attached separately as custom routes, from the dashboard, the API, the
          <span class="num">set_route</span> MCP tool or
          <span class="num">orva routes set</span>.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="bash"
          code={`$ curl -X POST https://orva.example.com/fn/<function_id> \\
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
    -d '{"name": "Orva"}'`}
        />
        <p>
          These prefixes are reserved and cannot be claimed by a custom route:
          <span class="num">/api/ /auth/ /fn/ /mcp/ /web/ /webhook/ /_orva/</span>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Config -->
      <section id="config">
        <h2>Function settings</h2>
        <p>
          Each function carries its own runtime, resource budget and network posture. The first five of these
          are <strong>spawn-scoped</strong>: a warm worker's environment is fixed when it spawns, so changing
          them takes effect on the next cold start rather than immediately.
        </p>

        <dl class="deflist">
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">runtime</span></dt>
            <dd><span class="num">node</span> or <span class="num">python</span>.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">memory_mb</span> / <span class="num">cpus</span></dt>
            <dd>Enforced by cgroup v2 on the sandbox, not advisory.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">timeout_ms</span></dt>
            <dd>
              Also surfaced to the handler as
              <span class="num">getRemainingTimeInMillis()</span> and
              <span class="num">get_remaining_time_in_millis()</span>.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">network_mode</span></dt>
            <dd>
              <span class="num">none</span> by default: an isolated network namespace with loopback only. <span
                class="num">egress</span
              > opts into outbound traffic and adds roughly 5 ms to a cold start. Without it, any outbound call
              fails
              <span class="num">ENETUNREACH</span>.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">auth_mode</span></dt>
            <dd>
              <span class="num">public</span> is the default and matches Cloudflare Workers and Vercel Functions.
              <span class="num">signed</span> reads its key from the function secret <span class="num"
                >ORVA_SIGNING_SECRET</span
              >.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">max_concurrency</span></dt>
            <dd>
              Caps in-flight invocations per function, so a runaway handler cannot exhaust whatever it calls
              downstream. <span class="num">0</span> means unlimited.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <!-- SDK -->
      <section id="sdk">
        <h2>The SDK</h2>
        <p>
          The <span class="num">orva</span> package is preinstalled in every sandbox. There is nothing to add to
          a manifest and no network call to fetch it. Note that anything reaching outside the sandbox still needs
          <span class="num">network_mode: egress</span>.
        </p>

        <h3>KV</h3>
        <p>
          A per-function JSON namespace on SQLite, with optional TTL. Batches are atomic: a validation or
          storage failure rolls the whole batch back. The Node SDK is promise-based; <strong
            >the Python SDK is synchronous</strong
          >.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="Node.js"
          lang="js"
          code={`const { kv } = require('orva')

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  // Omit ttlSeconds to keep expiry, 0 clears it.
  await kv.put('user:42', { name: 'Ada', tier: 'pro' }, { ttlSeconds: 3600 })

  const user = await kv.get('user:42', null)
  const pages = await kv.list({ prefix: 'page:', limit: 50 })
  await kv.delete('user:42')

  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(user) }
}`}
        />
        <CodeBlock
          label="Python"
          lang="python"
          code={`from orva import kv


def handler(event):
    kv.put("user:42", {"name": "Ada", "tier": "pro"}, ttl_seconds=3600)

    user = kv.get("user:42", None)
    pages = kv.list(prefix="page:", limit=50)
    kv.delete("user:42")

    return {"statusCode": 200, "body": user}`}
        />

        <h3>Function to function</h3>
        <p>
          <span class="num">invoke()</span> calls another function on the same instance, with call-depth limits
          so a cycle cannot spend the host.
          <span class="num">invoke_stream()</span> streams the response through instead of buffering it.
        </p>

        <h3>Background jobs</h3>
        <p>
          <span class="num">jobs.enqueue()</span> moves work off the request path. Pass an idempotency key so a
          retry does not run it twice.
        </p>

        <h3>Logs and spans</h3>
        <p>
          <span class="num">log</span> writes structured lines that land in the invocation log, and <span
            class="num">trace</span
          > opens custom spans that appear in the trace view. The import is <span class="num">log</span>, not <span
            class="num">logger</span
          >.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="Python"
          lang="python"
          code={`from orva import trace, log


def handler(event):
    with trace.span("fetch-user"):
        log.info("looking up", user_id=42)

    return {"statusCode": 200, "body": "ok"}`}
        />

        <h3>Webhooks</h3>
        <p>
          Deliveries are signed, and each runtime ships a verification helper. The
          <span class="num">x-orva-*</span> header namespace is server-set and stripped from inbound requests, so
          a caller cannot forge one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- CLI -->
      <section id="cli">
        <h2>CLI</h2>
        <p>
          A single static binary with no CGO, so it runs on a bare Alpine image. It reads
          <span class="num">~/.orva/config.yaml</span> for its endpoint and API key.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="bash"
          code={`# Key on stdin: the command line is visible via ps.
$ printf %s "$ORVA_KEY" | orva login --endpoint https://orva.example.com --api-key -

$ orva system health
$ orva deploy ./my-fn --name my-fn --runtime node
$ orva invoke my-fn --body '{"hello":"world"}'
$ orva logs my-fn --follow`}
        />
        <p>
          Beyond those, the surface covers <span class="num">functions</span>,
          <span class="num">deployments</span>, <span class="num">rollback</span>,
          <span class="num">diff</span>, <span class="num">kv</span>,
          <span class="num">secrets</span>, <span class="num">cron</span>,
          <span class="num">jobs</span>, <span class="num">keys</span>,
          <span class="num">traces</span>, <span class="num">executions</span>,
          <span class="num">activity</span>, <span class="num">pool</span>,
          <span class="num">routes</span>, <span class="num">channels</span>,
          <span class="num">firewall</span>, <span class="num">dns</span>,
          <span class="num">backup</span>, <span class="num">chat</span> and
          <span class="num">upgrade</span>. Run <span class="num">orva docs</span> for the full reference, which
          is embedded in the binary.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Server config -->
      <section id="server">
        <h2>Server config</h2>
        <p>The server is configured by environment variables only. There is no server config file.</p>
        <dl class="deflist">
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">ORVA_PORT</span></dt>
            <dd>
              Default <span class="num">8443</span>. Plain HTTP, no TLS. Put a reverse proxy in front of it
              for TLS.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">ORVA_HOST</span></dt>
            <dd>
              Default <span class="num">0.0.0.0</span>. Set <span class="num">127.0.0.1</span> to listen on loopback
              only, which is the recommendation behind a proxy.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">ORVA_DATA_DIR</span></dt>
            <dd>
              <span class="num">/var/lib/orva</span> under Docker,
              <span class="num">~/.orva</span> in development.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">ORVA_TRUSTED_PROXY</span></dt>
            <dd>
              Default <span class="num">false</span>. Turn it on only when a proxy in front of Orva actually
              sets <span class="num">X-Forwarded-For</span>, because it makes Orva trust that header.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="num">ORVA_LOG_LEVEL</span></dt>
            <dd>
              <span class="num">debug</span>, <span class="num">info</span> (default),
              <span class="num">warn</span> or <span class="num">error</span>.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p>
          The full table, including body limits, retention, session lifetime and the seccomp policy switch, is
          in
          <a class="link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/CONFIG.md`}>CONFIG.md</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Security -->
      <section id="security">
        <h2>Security model</h2>
        <p>Between a handler and the host kernel there are five layers:</p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="what is between user code and the host"
          copy={false}
          code={`host kernel
  └─ docker container "orva"
      └─ orvad (Go server)
          └─ nsjail process         unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER) drops effective caps
              └─ user namespace
                  ├─ chroot to runtime rootfs      read-only
                  ├─ /code bind-mount              read-only, function-private
                  ├─ tmpfs /tmp                    private, wiped on worker exit
                  ├─ cgroup v2                     memory + CPU + pids
                  ├─ seccomp filter                ~150 syscalls blocked
                  └─ user code (node / python)`}
        />
        <p>
          The gaps between those layers are enforced by the Linux kernel, not by Orva code. Orva configures
          the boundaries and trusts the kernel to hold them.
        </p>

        <h3>What is explicitly out of scope</h3>
        <p>
          Kernel-level zero-days and side-channel attacks. No seccomp filter is bulletproof, and the threat
          model says so rather than implying otherwise. Orva is also
          <strong>single-tenant</strong>: every function on an instance shares one host and one trust
          boundary, so it is not built to run code from people you do not trust.
        </p>

        <h3>Egress</h3>
        <p>
          Egress policy is per sandbox and fail-closed. The blocklist compiles into that sandbox's own network
          configuration, which the spawn refuses to start without. No host firewall table is ever created, so
          nothing Orva does can outlive the process that needed it.
        </p>

        <h3>Credentials</h3>
        <p>
          The SDK credential handed to a sandbox is bound to that worker and to a single spawn. It is released
          when the sandbox is reaped, so a redeploy genuinely invalidates a leaked copy rather than merely
          appearing to.
          <a class="link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/SECURITY.md`}>SECURITY.md</a> sets out each credential class
          and exactly what revoking it reaches.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- MCP -->
      <section id="mcp">
        <h2>MCP</h2>
        <p>
          Orva exposes <span class="num">73</span> tools over Model Context Protocol, so an agent can operate the
          instance the same way you do. A read-only key reaches
          <span class="num">28</span> of them. Authentication is OAuth 2.1 or a static bearer token.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="Claude Code"
          code={`$ claude mcp add --transport http orva https://orva.example.com/mcp/ \\
    --header "Authorization: Bearer $ORVA_KEY"`}
        />
        <p>
          Config snippets for Cursor, VS Code, Codex CLI, OpenCode, Zed, Windsurf, Claude Desktop and ChatGPT
          are in the reference. Agent channels let you bundle a set of functions and expose them as tools in
          their own right.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Troubleshooting -->
      <section id="trouble">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
        <dl class="deflist">
          <div>
            <dt>Every invocation fails, the server is up</dt>
            <dd>
              nsjail is missing. It must exist at
              <span class="num">/usr/local/bin/nsjail</span>; that path is hardcoded and PATH is not
              consulted.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>An outbound call fails with ENETUNREACH</dt>
            <dd>
              The function is on <span class="num">network_mode: none</span>, which is the default. Turn on
              "Allow outbound network" in its configuration.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>The handler returns 500 on a valid JSON POST</dt>
            <dd>
              <span class="num">event.body</span> is a raw string and has to be parsed. It is never an object, whatever
              the <span class="num">Content-Type</span> says.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>A config change did not take effect</dt>
            <dd>
              Runtime, memory, CPU, timeout and network mode are baked in when a worker spawns. A warm worker
              keeps the old environment until it is replaced.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Sandboxes will not start under Docker</dt>
            <dd>
              On the default runc runtime the container needs
              <span class="num">--pid host</span>, <span class="num">--cgroupns host</span> and
              <span class="num">--cap-add SYS_ADMIN</span>, plus a writable cgroup mount, to construct
              sandboxes at all.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  // Scroll spy for the table of contents.
  //
  // The rule is "the section you are currently reading", which is the LAST
  // section whose start has passed an activation line, not the first section
  // intersecting the viewport. Those differ constantly: a long section keeps
  // its heading far above the fold while the next one is still off screen, and
  // a first-intersecting check flips forward the instant the next heading peeks
  // in from the bottom, so the highlight runs ahead of the reader.
  const links = new Map<string, HTMLAnchorElement>();
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-toc-link]').forEach((a) => {
    links.set(a.dataset.tocLink!, a);
  });

  const sections = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.body section[id]')];

  if (sections.length && links.size) {
    let current: string | null = null;

    const setActive = (id: string | null) => {
      if (id === current) return;
      current = id;
      links.forEach((a, key) => {
        if (key === id) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
        else a.removeAttribute('aria-current');
      });
    };

    const offsetTop = (el: HTMLElement) => el.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.scrollY;

    const compute = () => {
      // The activation line sits below the top edge, so a section counts as
      // reached once it is comfortably on screen rather than at first pixel.
      const line = window.scrollY + window.innerHeight * 0.25;

      // At the end of the document the last section wins outright. Without this
      // a short final section can never cross the line, and the highlight
      // sticks on the second-to-last entry however far you scroll.
      if (window.innerHeight + window.scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2) {
        setActive(sections[sections.length - 1].id);
        return;
      }

      let active: string | null = null;
      for (const sec of sections) {
        if (offsetTop(sec) <= line) active = sec.id;
        else break;
      }
      setActive(active ?? sections[0].id);
    };

    let ticking = false;
    const onScroll = () => {
      if (ticking) return;
      ticking = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        compute();
        ticking = false;
      });
    };

    addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
    addEventListener('resize', onScroll, { passive: true });
    compute();
  }

  // Deep links on section headings.
  //
  // Added here rather than in the markup because it is an enhancement, not the
  // navigation: the contents list already reaches every section without it, so
  // nothing is lost when this does not run.
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.body section[id] > h2').forEach((h) => {
    const id = h.parentElement!.id;
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.className = 'anchor';
    a.href = '#' + id;
    a.setAttribute('aria-label', `Link to ${h.textContent!.trim()}`);
    a.textContent = '#';
    h.appendChild(a);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .docs-head {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--v-6) var(--pad) 0;
  }

  .docs-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    margin-top: var(--v-3);
  }

  .docs-head .lead {
    margin-top: var(--v-1);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .docs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--v-4);
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--v-4) var(--pad) var(--v-8);
  }

  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .docs {
      /* 6 columns of table of contents, 16 of prose, 2 of air. */
      grid-template-columns:
        calc(6 * var(--pitch) - var(--g))
        minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--pitch);
    }
  }

  .toc__in {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--v-3);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--v-0);
  }

  .toc nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--v-0);
    margin-top: var(--v-1);
  }

  /* Table-of-contents entries are navigation targets, not prose links, so the
     WCAG 2.5.8 inline-link exception does not cover them. */
  .toc nav a {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 26px;
    /* The shortest entry is "CLI", which sets about 22px wide. */
    min-width: 24px;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .toc nav a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Active section. Colour and weight, not a left stripe: DESIGN.md bans the
     side-stripe callout, and a bar here would be the only vertical rule on the
     page. */
  .toc nav a[aria-current='location'] {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Weight only where it cannot reflow.
     Desktop: the nav is a column flex container, so every link already
     stretches to the full sidebar width and going bold changes the text inside
     a box whose size is fixed. Nothing moves.
     Mobile: the nav is a wrapping row, where a bold label is a wider box and
     can rewrap the whole list mid-scroll. There the active state is carried by
     colour alone. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .toc nav a[aria-current='location'] {
      font-weight: 600;
    }
  }

  .toc__note {
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-top: var(--v-2);
    padding-top: var(--v-1);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bd-hair);
  }

  /* Below lg the table of contents is a plain block above the prose rather than
     a collapsed accordion. Ten links do not need a disclosure widget, and a
